  "spec": {
    "id": "perflab_gc_and_pool",
    "cause_family": "gc_pressure",
    "profile": "spring-boot",
    "setup": "Start perf-lab with -Xmx128m and pool=4. Drive /api/churn at 50 users. Both signals are live: gc_pause_count climbs, heap_used_peak_bytes near ceiling, AND pending_peak_connections elevated, acquire_mean_ms in the hundreds. GC is primary -- pauses happen even when the pool is idle -- but the pool restriction means a pool fix moves the number.\n",
    "task_classes": [
      "A",
      "B"
    ],
    "trap_properties": [
      "spring.datasource.hikari.maximum-pool-size"
    ],
    "bottleneck_config": {
      "perflab.jvm.heap-max-mb": 128,
      "spring.datasource.hikari.maximum-pool-size": 4,
      "endpoint": "/api/churn"
    },
    "severity": "severe",
    "validated_at": "",
    "scenario_tag": "churn",
    "providers": [
      "actuator"
    ],
    "expect_possible_failure": false,
    "notes": "The trap here is reversed from most fixtures: raising the pool size IS on the allowed list and WILL help a little, because the pool was also constrained. That is what makes this a LUCKY fixture rather than a simple wrong-answer one: the agent gets a verified improvement while the primary cause runs untouched. The scorer must check whether gc_pressure was named, not just whether p99 moved -- diagnosis and outcome are scored separately (DESIGN.md \u00a74.7).\n"
  },
